<TopicPage slug="subjects/physics/expert/general-relativity">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    General relativity (1915) is Einstein's answer to an awkward question: if special relativity
    says "simultaneous" depends on the observer, what does <em>gravity</em> do to that story?
    His answer redefines gravity out of existence: mass and energy <strong>curve
    spacetime</strong>, and objects simply follow the straightest available paths through the
    curved geometry. Planets don't feel a pull; they coast along dented spacetime.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="gravitational time dilation — clocks run slower deep in the curve; at the horizon (x = 1) they stop" widget="plotter" config={gravCfg} />

  <p>
    Time itself is the sensitive instrument. Near a one-solar-mass black hole's horizon, a clock
    ticks at nearly zero compared with a distant observer. In the weak gravity of Earth the
    effect is tiny — but real: your feet age slightly less than your head, and GPS satellites
    must correct both this (clocks run faster up there, +45 µs/day) and their speed (−7 µs/day)
    or navigation drifts kilometres per day.
  </p>

  <h2>The evidence trail</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>1919 — starlight bends:</strong> Eddington's eclipse expedition measured stars shifting near the eclipsed Sun by the exact angle Einstein predicted. He became the first celebrity scientist overnight.</li>
    <li><strong>Mercury's orbit:</strong> its perihelion drifts 43 arcseconds per century beyond Newton — general relativity nails it with no tuning. Einstein called it the most thrilling moment of his life.</li>
    <li><strong>2015 — gravitational waves:</strong> LIGO heard two black holes merge 1.3 billion light-years away, spacetime ringing like a drum — a prediction from 1916 finally detected, Nobel 2017.</li>
    <li><strong>2019 & 2022 — the pictures:</strong> Event Horizon Telescope imaged the shadows of the M87 and Milky Way central black holes — the strongest-curvature regions tested so far.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>The equation, in spirit</h2>
  <p>
    G(geometry) = 8πT(matter-energy) — one line: "matter tells spacetime how to curve; spacetime
    tells matter how to move" (Wheeler). The black holes, the Big Bang, gravitational lensing and
    the expanding universe are all solutions to this one equation. It remains undefeated by any
    experiment — though quantum mechanics and it still refuse to merge, which is the biggest open
    problem in physics.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Einstein (1915); Eddington (1919); LIGO/Virgo, PRL (2016); EHT, ApJL (2019).</p>
</TopicPage>
